<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitution Act, 1867</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as British North America Act, British North America Act 1867, BNA Act</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitution Act, 1867, originally the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> Act, is the act of the British Parliament that created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> on 1 July 1867 by uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and the Province of Canada, divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. It set up a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a> and divided powers between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> and the provinces, and it remains the core of the Canadian constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1867.jpg" alt="Constitution Act, 1867" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Statute enacted by the Parliament of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland; Published by Hunter, Rose in Ottawa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_North_America_Act,_1867.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The preamble records that New Brunswick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> had asked to form one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> with a constitution similar in principle to that of the United Kingdom. The act made Canada the name of the new federation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> its seat of government, left executive authority vested in the Queen, exercised by the Governor General with a Privy Council, and created a Parliament of the monarch, a Senate and a House of Commons. Senators were appointed rather than elected, 72 at the union, in equal regional divisions, and money bills had to begin in the Commons.</p><p>Sections 91 and 92 list the subjects of federal and provincial law: Parliament may legislate for the peace, order and good government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and on criminal law, marriage and divorce, and Indians and lands reserved for the Indians, while the provinces hold property and civil rights and the administration of justice. Education went to the provinces with protections for denominational schools, reflecting the Protestant and Catholic quarrels of the time. Section 133 allows English and French in the federal Parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> legislature and their courts, and section 146 provided for admitting further colonies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The courts have read much into the act's brief text. The Privy Council held that the peace, order and good government power is a limited federal competence rather than a general residue, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-court-of-canada">Supreme Court of Canada</a> relied on the preamble in 1993 on parliamentary privilege and in the Provincial Judges Reference of 1997 to hold that judicial independence is guaranteed. Until the decision in Edwards v <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> (AG) of 1929 the provision on appointing senators was read as excluding women. Section 91(24) allowed the government to act as if the treaties between Indigenous peoples and the Crown before Confederation, such as the Treaty of Niagara of 1764, did not exist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The act has no written bill of rights. When the constitution was patriated in 1982, the British North America Acts were renamed the Constitution Acts and section 92A added, giving the provinces greater control over non-renewable natural resources. The day the act took effect, 1 July 1867, is kept as Canada Day, called Dominion Day before 1982.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constitution Act, 1867” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British Parliament passed the <b>British North America Act</b>, and on 1 July 1867 the Dominion of Canada came into being, uniting Nova Scotia, New Brunswick and the Province of Canada, which was divided into Ontario and Quebec.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Within the British Empire, Canada federated under the <b>British North America Act</b> of 1867 and Australia in 1901, after referendums in each colony, while India, whose constitution of 1950 calls it a “Union of States”, Pakistan, Nigeria and Malaya became federations around the time of their independence.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
